WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.855
Tarjan's DFS classifies undirected edges into tree edges that discover new vertices and back-edges that jump to visited ancestors.

00:00:09.855 --> 00:00:19.255
Record tin[u] when vertex u is first entered, and maintain low[u] as the earliest discovery time reachable from u's subtree.

00:00:19.255 --> 00:00:25.320
Ignore the direct edge back to the immediate parent unless parallel edges exist.

00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:32.830
A tree edge from u to child v is a bridge if and only if low[v] is strictly greater than tin[u].

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:40.076
Strict inequality means no vertex in v's subtree has a back-edge reaching u or an ancestor of u.

00:00:40.076 --> 00:00:44.303
Deleting a bridge splits the connected component in two.

00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:55.708
A non-root vertex u is an articulation point if it has at least one child subtree v with low[v] greater than or equal to tin[u].

00:00:55.708 --> 00:01:02.575
Because v can only reach u and not above u, removing u disconnects v's subtree.

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:10.223
The DFS root is an articulation point exactly when it has two or more DFS tree children.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:17.987
Contracting every bridge-connected component into a single super-node turns any connected graph into a tree of bridges.

00:01:17.987 --> 00:01:24.404
Paths in this condensed tree reveal which edges must be traversed by every route between two vertices.

00:01:24.404 --> 00:01:29.311
You can then apply standard tree DP or LCA queries on the condensed structure.
